INSERT INTO `ai_tutor_interactions` VALUES (1,1,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','What is PHP?','PHP stands for Hypertext Preprocessor, which is a bit of a twist because it\'s a recursive acronym where the first letter stands for the acronym itself! It\'s a popular server-side scripting language designed specifically for web development but also used as a general-purpose programming language.\n\nPHP code is executed on the server, generating HTML which is then sent to the client\'s web browser. This means that PHP can be used to create dynamic content that can change or adapt depending on user interactions, database modifications, or other factors when a webpage is viewed. \n\nOne of the strengths of PHP is its ease of use for newcomers, along with its extensive capabilities for professional developers, making it a versatile choice for developing websites and web applications. It integrates well with various databases, such as MySQL, and has a vast ecosystem of frameworks and libraries to speed up development and enhance functionality.\n\nConsidering your current focus, how do you see PHP fitting into your learning goals or projects?','[]',NULL,'2025-12-25 14:42:30'),(2,10,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','How can you help me learn?','I\'m here to guide you through your learning journey with a supportive and engaging approach. Here\'s how I can help:\n\n1. **Guidance on Concepts**: If you\'re struggling to understand a particular concept, I\'ll provide explanations, ask you guiding questions to stimulate your thinking, and use examples or analogies that relate to the material you\'re learning. This way, you can build a deeper understanding.\n\n2. **Encouragement and Feedback**: Learning can be challenging, and I\'m here to encourage you every step of the way. I\'ll celebrate your progress and provide constructive feedback to help you keep moving forward.\n\n3. **Problem-Solving Strategies**: When you\'re tackling assignments or projects, I won\'t give you the answers directly. Instead, I\'ll guide you towards finding the solutions yourself by asking thought-provoking questions and suggesting strategies to approach the problem.\n\n4. **Resource Suggestions**: If you need additional resources to supplement your learning, I can recommend articles, videos, and exercises that align with your current topics.\n\n5. **Study Habits and Techniques**: I can also offer advice on effective study habits, time management, and how to approach learning new material in a structured and efficient way.\n\nRemember, your curiosity and willingness to learn are your greatest assets. Feel free to ask me anything related to your course material, and I\'ll do my best to assist you. What specific area are you currently focusing on in your learning?','[]',NULL,'2025-12-25 14:48:42'),(3,10,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Can you give me a practical example?','Certainly! Let\'s say you\'re currently learning about algorithms in computer science, specifically focusing on sorting algorithms. A practical example could be understanding how the bubble sort algorithm works. Instead of just explaining the steps, we can approach it with a real-world analogy and then dive into the algorithm itself.\n\n### Real-World Analogy:\nImagine you have a row of books on a shelf, and you want to organize them by height, from shortest to tallest. You start at one end and compare each pair of adjacent books. Every time you find a pair where the taller book is before the shorter one, you swap them. You keep moving along the shelf, comparing and swapping, until you reach the end. Then, you repeat the process from the beginning until you make a pass without needing any swaps. At that point, the books are sorted.\n\n### Connecting to Bubble Sort:\nThis process mirrors the bubble sort algorithm with an array of numbers. Here\'s a simplified explanation and guiding questions to think through:\n\n1. **Concept**: The bubble sort algorithm sorts an array by repeatedly stepping through the list, comparing adjacent elements, and swapping them if they are in the wrong order. The pass through the list is repeated until the list is sorted.\n\n2. **Why \"Bubble\"?**: Imagine each number \"bubbling\" up to its correct position with each pass through the array, similar to how bubbles rise to the surface of water. \n\n3. **Efficiency Question**: Considering the process, do you think bubble sort is more efficient for sorting small or large arrays? Why?\n\n4. **Code Connection**: If we were to translate the bookshelf analogy into a bubble sort algorithm for an array of numbers, what would be the first step in coding this? Hint: think about how you\'d need to compare each element with its neighbor.\n\nThis approach, starting with an analogy and then connecting it back to the technical concept, is designed to make complex ideas more relatable and easier to grasp. How does that analogy sit with you? Does it help clarify how the bubble sort algorithm works?','[]',NULL,'2025-12-25 14:49:25'),(4,10,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Do you know the course I am learning now?','I don\'t have real-time access to your current course details or progress, but if you tell me what you\'re learning or focusing on right now, I can tailor my guidance and support to match your needs. Whether it\'s a topic in computer science, mathematics, a programming language, or any other subject, just let me know what you\'re working on, and I\'ll do my best to assist you. What topic or assignment are you currently exploring?','[]',NULL,'2025-12-25 14:54:52'),(5,10,'',NULL,NULL,3311,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Do you know the course I am learning now','Yes, you\'re currently learning about Frontend Development, specifically focusing on the module about Frontend Best Practices. Your current lesson is on Code Organization. This is a great area to delve into as it covers the essentials of how to structure and maintain your code effectively, which is crucial for building scalable and maintainable web applications. How are you finding the course so far? Is there a particular aspect of code organization you\'re curious about?','{\"lesson_id\":3311,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2025-12-25 14:58:34'),(6,10,'',NULL,NULL,3311,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Can you explain more about learners will demonstrate the key aspects of code organization in practical scenarios.','Of course! Demonstrating the key aspects of code organization in practical scenarios involves applying the principles you\'ve learned about structuring and managing code to real-world projects or exercises. This can take various forms, but let\'s explore a few key aspects and see how they might be demonstrated practically:\n\n### 1. **Modularization**\n- **What it is:** Breaking down your code into smaller, reusable pieces (modules) that handle specific tasks.\n- **Demonstration in practice:** You might create a web page where JavaScript functionality is divided into modules; for example, one module for handling user inputs, another for processing data, and another for rendering results on the UI. This makes your code more readable and easier to debug.\n\n### 2. **Naming Conventions**\n- **What it is:** Consistently naming your variables, functions, and classes in a way that reveals their purpose.\n- **Demonstration in practice:** When working on a project, adhere to a clear naming convention (such as camelCase for variables and functions, PascalCase for classes) and ensure that the names give an immediate understanding of what a variable/function/class does. For instance, a function that fetches user data might be named `fetchUserData()`.\n\n### 3. **File Structure**\n- **What it is:** Organizing your project files in a logical hierarchy.\n- **Demonstration in practice:** In a web project, you could organize your files by separating HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files into different folders. Further, JavaScript files could be organized by their role in the application (e.g., UI components, utilities, services).\n\n### 4. **Commenting and Documentation**\n- **What it is:** Adding comments within your code and maintaining documentation that explains how your code works.\n- **Demonstration in practice:** Write meaningful comments that explain why you\'re doing something in a particular way, not just what you\'re doing. For larger projects, maintain a README file or similar documentation that guides others on how to set up, use, and contribute to your project.\n\n### 5. **Consistent Style**\n- **What it is:** Following a consistent coding style regarding indentation, spacing, file naming, etc.\n- **Demonstration in practice:** Adopt a style guide (like Airbnb\'s JavaScript style guide) and apply it throughout your project. Tools like ESLint can help enforce these styles automatically.\n\n### 6. **Version Control**\n- **What it is:** Using version control systems like Git to manage changes to your codebase.\n- **Demonstration in practice:** Regularly commit your changes to a Git repository with clear, descriptive commit messages. This not only backs up your work but also makes it easier to collaborate with others and track down when and why changes were made.\n\nBy integrating these practices into your projects, you not only improve the quality and maintainability of your code but also demonstrate a professional level of code organization. Which of these aspects would you like to explore further through a practical scenario?','{\"lesson_id\":3311,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2025-12-25 14:59:33'),(7,10,'',1,NULL,3311,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Hi','Hello! How can I assist you with your Frontend Development course today? Whether it\'s diving deeper into code organization techniques or tackling a different topic altogether, I\'m here to help. What\'s on your mind?','{\"lesson_id\":3311,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":1}',NULL,'2025-12-25 15:00:14'),(8,10,'',1,NULL,3311,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Hi','Hello again! If you\'ve got any questions or need help with something specific, feel free to let me know. Whether it\'s about code organization, a different part of your Frontend Development course, or even just a general coding question, I\'m here to help.','{\"lesson_id\":3311,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":1}',NULL,'2025-12-25 15:00:47'),(9,10,'',NULL,NULL,3311,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','What should I focus on next?','Given where you are in your learning journey, focusing on applying the principles of code organization in practical scenarios would be highly beneficial. This not only solidifies what you\'ve learned but also prepares you for more complex topics ahead. Here are a few suggestions on what to focus on next:\n\n### 1. **Practice with Projects**\nStart a small project or contribute to an existing one. This could be anything from a simple to-do list application to contributing to open source projects that interest you. Applying code organization principles in real projects will help you understand their importance and how they affect the scalability and maintainability of a codebase.\n\n### 2. **Code Refactoring**\nTake a piece of existing code and work on refactoring it. This means restructuring the code without changing its external behavior to improve its internal structure. Focus on aspects like modularization, improving file structure, and ensuring the code follows naming conventions and style guides. This exercise is invaluable for understanding the practical benefits of organized code.\n\n### 3. **Learn Version Control Systems**\nIf you haven\'t already, diving deeper into version control systems, particularly Git, is crucial. Being proficient in Git allows you to better manage your code changes, collaborate with others, and understand the flow of projects in the real world. It\'s a key skill in a developer\'s toolkit.\n\n### 4. **Explore CSS Methodologies**\nFor the frontend specifically, understanding and applying CSS methodologies like BEM (Block Element Modifier) can significantly improve how you organize your CSS code. This leads to more maintainable and scalable stylesheets.\n\n### 5. **Participate in Code Reviews**\nEngaging in code reviews, either by having your code reviewed by more experienced developers or by reviewing others\' code, can provide deep insights into best practices and new ways of organizing code. It\'s also a great way to learn from real-world coding styles and techniques.\n\n### 6. **Study Architectural Patterns**\nStart familiarizing yourself with architectural patterns used in frontend development, such as MVC (Model-View-Controller) or Flux. Understanding these patterns will help you in organizing code logically, especially as you move on to more complex applications.\n\nRemember, the key to mastering frontend development is consistent practice and seeking feedback. Every project you work on or piece of code you refactor is a step forward in your learning journey. Is there a specific area you\'re excited to explore next or any concept you\'d like more clarity on?','{\"lesson_id\":3311,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2025-12-25 15:05:30'),(10,10,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Explain the main concept of this lesson','The main concept of your current lesson on Code Organization in Frontend Development revolves around structuring and managing your code in a way that makes it readable, maintainable, and scalable. Let\'s break this down into several key components to understand the overarching concept better:\n\n### Modularization\nThe practice of breaking down your code into smaller, manageable pieces or modules. Each module should focus on a single responsibility or feature. This not only makes your code more reusable and easier to read but also simplifies debugging and testing.\n\n### Naming Conventions\nAdopting clear and consistent naming conventions for variables, functions, classes, and files. Proper naming helps convey the purpose of a code block at a glance, making your code more understandable and maintainable.\n\n### File Structure\nOrganizing your project files in a logical and hierarchical manner. A well-thought-out file structure makes it easier for developers to navigate and understand the project, as well as integrate new features without disrupting the existing codebase.\n\n### Commenting and Documentation\nWriting meaningful comments and maintaining up-to-date documentation. Comments should explain \"why\" something is done a certain way, rather than \"what\" is being done, which the code itself should convey. Documentation serves as a guide for anyone new to the project, detailing how to set up, use, and contribute to the project.\n\n### Consistent Styling\nFollowing a consistent style guide for your code. This includes rules for formatting, such as indentation, spaces, line length, etc. Consistent styling makes the code aesthetically uniform and, thus, easier to read and understand.\n\n### Version Control\nUsing version control systems, like Git, to manage changes to your codebase. This allows for tracking changes, collaborating with others more effectively, and maintaining a history of your project\'s development.\n\nThe essence of these concepts is to ensure that your code is not just functional but also clean, organized, and approachable by others (and yourself in the future). This is crucial in a collaborative environment and for the long-term sustainability of the project. How do you feel about applying these concepts in your projects? Are there any areas you\'d like to delve deeper into?','{\"lesson_id\":null,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2025-12-25 15:06:51'),(11,10,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Can you give me a practical example?','Certainly! Let\'s take a practical example of creating a simple web application to understand how code organization principles are applied. Imagine we\'re building a \"Task Manager\" app where users can add, delete, and mark tasks as completed. Here\'s how we might apply code organization principles to this project:\n\n### 1. **Modularization**\nWe divide our JavaScript code into modules:\n- **taskManager.js**: Handles the core logic of adding, deleting, and toggling tasks.\n- **uiController.js**: Manages the UI components, like input fields, buttons, and the task list display.\n- **storage.js**: Manages saving and retrieving tasks from the browser\'s localStorage for persistence.\n\n### 2. **Naming Conventions**\nWe use clear, descriptive names for our functions and variables:\n- Functions in `taskManager.js` might include `addTask(taskDetails)`, `deleteTask(taskId)`, and `toggleTaskCompleted(taskId)`.\n- In `uiController.js`, we could have `renderTasks(tasks)`, `clearInputField()`, and `showNotification(message)`.\n\n### 3. **File Structure**\nOur project directory could look like this:\n```\n/task-manager\n    /css\n        style.css\n    /js\n        taskManager.js\n        uiController.js\n        storage.js\n    index.html\n    README.md\n```\nThis structure keeps our styles, scripts, and HTML files organized.\n\n### 4. **Commenting and Documentation**\n- **Comments**: In `taskManager.js`, we might comment on the `addTask` function to explain the logic behind task ID generation.\n- **Documentation**: Our `README.md` includes setup instructions, features of the app, and how to contribute to the project.\n\n### 5. **Consistent Styling**\nWe decide on a coding style (e.g., Airbnb\'s JavaScript style guide) and use tools like Prettier to format our code automatically, ensuring consistency across all files.\n\n### 6. **Version Control**\nWe initialize a Git repository in our project directory. As we complete features or make significant changes, we commit with descriptive messages:\n- Initial commit with project structure.\n- Add task addition functionality.\n- Implement task deletion.\n\nBy applying these principles, not only do we make our \"Task Manager\" app functional, but we also ensure that the code is organized, readable, and maintainable. This approach benefits us and others who might work on the project in the future.\n\nWould you like to dive deeper into any of these aspects, or is there a specific part of the process you\'d like to explore further?','{\"lesson_id\":null,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2025-12-25 15:07:26'),(12,10,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','hello','Hello! How can I assist you today? If there\'s a specific topic or question you have in mind, feel free to share it!','{\"lesson_id\":null,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2025-12-25 15:12:55'),(13,10,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','How does this relate to what we learned before?','Let\'s connect the dots between what we\'ve previously discussed and our example of organizing code for a \"Task Manager\" app. Before, we delved into the principles of code organization, which include modularization, naming conventions, file structure, commenting and documentation, consistent styling, and version control. Each of these principles plays a crucial role in developing maintainable, scalable, and collaborative projects. \n\nIn the \"Task Manager\" app example, we applied these principles practically:\n\n- **Modularization**: We divided the application into distinct modules (taskManager.js, uiController.js, storage.js), each responsible for a separate concern. This approach echoes our discussion on separating functionalities to enhance readability and maintainability.\n\n- **Naming Conventions**: By choosing clear, descriptive names for functions and variables, we implemented the principle of using naming conventions to improve code clarity and developer communication.\n\n- **File Structure**: Organizing the project into directories for CSS, JavaScript, and HTML files demonstrates the file structure principle, making the project more navigable and intuitive.\n\n- **Commenting and Documentation**: Including comments in our code and maintaining a README.md for documentation directly applies our discussion on the importance of explaining the \"why\" behind code choices and providing setup instructions and project overviews.\n\n- **Consistent Styling**: Adopting a style guide and using formatting tools ensures that our code remains consistent and adheres to best practices, reinforcing the principle of consistent styling for better readability and team collaboration.\n\n- **Version Control**: Utilizing Git for version control allows us to manage changes effectively, collaborate with others, and maintain a history of our project\'s development, underlining the need for version control in code organization.\n\nBy connecting these principles with a practical example, it reinforces the relevance of what we\'ve learned and shows how these concepts are applied in real-world scenarios. This not only aids in solidifying your understanding but also prepares you for implementing these best practices in your projects.\n\nDoes this help make the connection clearer, or is there a specific aspect you\'d like to explore further?','{\"lesson_id\":null,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2025-12-25 15:13:24'),(14,10,'',NULL,NULL,3257,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Hi','Hello! How can I help you with your journey in learning about Web Browsers and Rendering today?','{\"lesson_id\":3257,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2025-12-30 16:15:35'),(15,10,'',NULL,NULL,3257,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Can you give me a practical example?','Certainly! Let\'s consider a real-world scenario to understand web browsers and rendering better.\n\nImagine you\'re visiting a cafe\'s website to check their menu. When you type the website\'s URL into your web browser and press Enter, here\'s a simplified version of what happens:\n\n1. **Your Web Browser**: This is the tool you use to navigate the internet, such as Chrome, Firefox, or Safari. It sends a request to the server where the cafe\'s website is hosted, asking for the website\'s data.\n\n2. **The Server**: This is a computer that hosts the cafe\'s website. It responds to your browser\'s request by sending back the data needed to display the website. This data includes HTML (the structure), CSS (the styling), and JavaScript (the interactive elements).\n\n3. **Rendering**: Once your browser receives this data, it needs to turn it into a visual and interactive web page that you can interact with. This process is called rendering. The browser interprets the HTML to create the page\'s structure, applies the CSS for style, and runs JavaScript for any interactive elements, like an animated menu or a form to make a reservation.\n\n4. **The Final Product**: The result is the cafe\'s webpage displayed on your browser, showing you the menu, images of the cafe, and maybe an option to book a table. You didn\'t see the code that made up the page; you saw the browser\'s rendered version of that code, making it understandable and interactive for you.\n\nThis example illustrates the fundamental roles of web browsers (like Chrome or Firefox) and the rendering process in translating code from the server into the interactive web pages we interact with daily. Does this help clarify the concepts for you?','{\"lesson_id\":3257,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2025-12-30 16:16:32'),(16,10,'',NULL,NULL,3258,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','hello','Hello! How can I assist you with your lesson on Developer Tools Overview today?','{\"lesson_id\":3258,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2026-01-13 16:21:35'),(17,10,'',NULL,NULL,3258,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Can you explain more about list developer tools overview concepts and principles.','Absolutely, I\'d be happy to delve into the concepts and principles of developer tools overview with you. Developer tools, often referred to as DevTools, are software applications that assist developers in creating, testing, and debugging their code. They are integral to web development and can significantly enhance productivity and code quality. Let\'s break down some of the key concepts and principles:\n\n### 1. **Integrated Development Environments (IDEs)**\n- **Concept**: An IDE is a comprehensive tool that provides a wide range of features for software development beyond just writing code. They often include a source code editor, build automation tools, and a debugger.\n- **Principle**: The principle behind IDEs is to provide developers with a single application that has all the necessary tools for software development, enhancing efficiency and reducing the need for context switching.\n\n### 2. **Version Control Systems (VCS)**\n- **Concept**: Version control is a system that records changes to a file or set of files over time so that you can recall specific versions later. Git, for example, is a widely used VCS.\n- **Principle**: The principle here is to manage changes and collaboration in a project efficiently, allowing multiple developers to work together without losing track of who did what and when.\n\n### 3. **Debugging Tools**\n- **Concept**: Debugging tools are used to test and debug code. These tools can help identify, trace, and fix issues within the source code.\n- **Principle**: The underlying principle is to minimize the time spent identifying and resolving defects in the code, improving the overall quality and reliability of the software.\n\n### 4. **Build Tools**\n- **Concept**: Build tools automate the process of converting source code files into standalone software artifacts that can be run on a computer.\n- **Principle**: Automation of the build process increases productivity by reducing manual work and helps in maintaining consistency across builds.\n\n### 5. **Testing Frameworks**\n- **Concept**: Testing frameworks provide a set of guidelines and functionalities for writing and executing test cases on software applications.\n- **Principle**: The principle is to ensure that the software meets its design and behaves as intended, through systematic testing.\n\n### 6. **Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD)**\n- **Concept**: CI/CD is a method to frequently deliver apps to customers by introducing automation into the stages of app development.\n- **Principle**: The key principle is to automate the software delivery process to make it reliable, quick, and efficient.\n\n### 7. **Performance and Optimization Tools**\n- **Concept**: These tools analyze the performance of web applications and identify bottlenecks or areas for improvement.\n- **Principle**: The aim is to enhance the speed, responsiveness, and overall user experience of web applications.\n\nWould you like to dive deeper into any of these areas or discuss how they apply in practical scenarios?','{\"lesson_id\":3258,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2026-01-13 16:22:40'),(18,10,'',NULL,NULL,3258,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Real worl scenario','Certainly! Let\'s consider a real-world scenario where a team of developers is working on a web application project. This scenario will illustrate how developer tools are applied in various stages of the project, showcasing their importance and utility.\n\n### Scenario: Developing a New Web Application\n\n**Stage 1: Planning and Setup**\n- The team decides on the project\'s scope and the technologies to be used. They choose an **Integrated Development Environment (IDE)** like Visual Studio Code for writing and managing their code because it supports HTML, CSS, and JavaScript out of the box and offers useful extensions for web development.\n\n**Stage 2: Version Control Setup**\n- To collaborate effectively, they initialize a **Version Control System (VCS)** repository using Git and host it on GitHub. This allows team members to work on different features simultaneously, track changes, and merge their work without conflicts.\n\n**Stage 3: Writing Code**\n- As the developers write code, they frequently use the debugging tools and console within their IDE and the **browser\'s developer tools** to test and debug. These tools help them inspect elements, test responsive designs, and debug JavaScript in real-time.\n\n**Stage 4: Building and Testing**\n- Before committing changes, developers use **build tools** like Webpack to bundle their JavaScript files and preprocessors. They also rely on **testing frameworks** like Jest to write and run unit tests, ensuring that individual components of the application behave as expected.\n\n**Stage 5: Continuous Integration and Deployment**\n- Once the code is pushed to their VCS, a **Continuous Integration (CI)** service like Jenkins or GitHub Actions automatically builds and runs tests on the application. If everything passes, the CI tool can automatically deploy the application to a staging environment using **Continuous Deployment (CD)** practices, allowing for quick feedback on the working application.\n\n**Stage 6: Performance Optimization**\n- After deployment, the team uses **performance and optimization tools** like Google\'s Lighthouse to analyze the web application\'s performance, accessibility, and SEO. Based on the reports, the developers make necessary optimizations to improve load times, responsiveness, and overall user experience.\n\n**Stage 7: Maintenance and Updates**\n- Post-launch, the team continues to use their developer tools for ongoing maintenance, implementing new features, and optimizing the application. The VCS allows for tracking changes and managing updates efficiently, while CI/CD ensures that new changes are automatically tested and deployed without disrupting the live application.\n\n### Conclusion\nThis scenario demonstrates how developer tools streamline the process of web development from conception to deployment and beyond. By leveraging these tools, the team can work more efficiently, collaborate effectively, and maintain high standards of quality and performance in their web application.','{\"lesson_id\":3258,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2026-01-13 16:24:55'),(19,10,'',NULL,NULL,3258,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','who is the current president of Ghana?','As of my last update in December 2023, the President of Ghana is Nana Akufo-Addo. He has been in office since January 7, 2017, after winning the December 2016 election. Please note that this information might change based on elections and other political developments, so it\'s always a good idea to check the most current sources for the latest updates. \n\nIs there anything else you\'d like to know?','{\"lesson_id\":3258,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2026-01-13 16:30:33'),(20,10,'',NULL,NULL,3271,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','hello','Hello! How can I assist you with your Frontend Development course today?','{\"lesson_id\":3271,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2026-01-13 16:32:29'),(21,10,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'question','Give me a hint to help me understand','Of course! Let\'s start with a foundational aspect of Frontend Development. One of the core technologies you need to understand is HTML (HyperText Markup Language), along with CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) and JavaScript. These three are the building blocks of the web.\n\n### HTML\nHTML is used to structure content on the web. Think of it as the skeleton of a webpage.\n\n**Hint:** If you were to create a simple webpage, what are the basic HTML elements you think you would need to include? Consider elements like `<html>`, `<head>`, `<title>`, and `<body>`.\n\n### CSS\nCSS is used for styling and visually formatting HTML elements. It\'s like the clothing that dresses up the HTML skeleton.\n\n**Hint:** How would you change the color of a paragraph text in HTML using CSS? Think about the `style` attribute that you can use directly in an HTML tag or external CSS with selectors.\n\n### JavaScript\nJavaScript makes the webpage interactive. It\'s like the muscles that allow the skeleton to move and react.\n\n**Hint:** Can you think of a simple JavaScript function that changes the text of an HTML element when a user clicks a button?\n\nBy starting to understand these concepts and how they interact with one another, you\'ll be on a solid path in your Frontend Development journey. \n\nWould you like to dive deeper into any of these areas or have specific questions on them?','{\"lesson_id\":null,\"practical_id\":null,\"quiz_id\":null,\"course_id\":null}',NULL,'2026-01-13 17:05:48');
